Mandevil the lawful male human Caveman

After ascending Barbarian more than month ago, I moved my effort to
Caveman.
This turned out to take me much longer than I expected. It took me 33
games
to ascend my Caveman and I suffered long array of YASD's along the
way,
including rather memorable death from Dispater in Orcus Town, unlucky
instakill from black dragon in Mines, stoning from eaten unidentified
cockatrice egg, choking from eating a troll and so on.

- in shop at dlvl 2 found gloves later identified as blessed +1
gauntlets of
power
- did Mines after Sokoban (with BoH)
- the temple in the Mine Town was coaligned
- got unremarkable bones in Minetown
- got my own(!) bones in the Mines dlvl 13, where I was killed by two
sergeants - again, nothing game changing
- found polytrap deep in the Mines, after lot of tries my pet dog
ended up
as a vampire (and quickly levelled up to a deadly vampire lord)
- on dlvl 4 I killed a werewolf who dropped +4 orcish helm!
- on dlvl 12 found uncursed ring of levitation
- I came across several large leprechaun halls, so soon I had so much
gold
that I had to stash it
- also in the way were two large barracks, but I postoponed clearing
those,
since I still had no reflection and the chance of soldier with wand of
death
is not that low, given their amount
- the titan at Medusa gave me hard time and among others he summoned
two mind
flayers, one of them managing to suck me and cause amnesia
- some bloody sucker zapped wand of striking just as I was on Castle
drawbridge, fortunately I was wearing amulet of life saving
- after clearing the Castle I wished for shield of reflection (I
already had
GDSM from slain dragon and Cavemen cannot two-weapon)
- the Quest was rather unremarkable (except for the fact that I fought
the
Chromatic Dragon wearing blindfold by accident)
- Gehennom was fairly straightforward and I found my only magic
marker
there on dlvl 31, also found two magic lamps
- killing Rodney was fun, since he immediately stole my quest
artifact,
obtaining magic resistance; it took me two charges from the wand of
death to
realize this (with the help of #nethack); then he insisted on floating
over
his tower's moat and I had no wand of cold, so I had to fight him only
when
he was above firm land
- after invocation, I levelported to the Sanctum (of course, had to
wish for
the ring of teleport control) and by pure luck ended up in against the
wall
with the secret door; before I reached it, I had to handle Rodney
again;
after getting the Amulet i quaffed cursed gain level and I was out --
I
spent in the Moloch's Sanctum just 22 turns
- the way out of Gehennom was unexpectedly fast -- mysterious force
kicked
in only twice and threw me one level back only; compare this to
chaotic
player Audelyn going the same way along with me for whom the
mysterious
force triggered about 20 times -- strange
- the Planes were quick and straightforward matter, as I was using the
Sceptre's conflict all the time since the Plane of Air (except that I
found
the hard way that casting forgotten spell has chance of only stunning
you,
leaving you unconfused)
- upon arrival at the Astral Plane, I rubbed my magic lamp and wished
for a
rubber chicken; the only snafu was, that in front of entrance to the
first
temple there was player character, who was using his Vorpal Blade -- I
took
no risks and zapped him with wand of death and successfully collected
the
blessed greased +4 Vorpal Blade; the second altar I tried was the
right one
-- only needed to kill riders twice

In summary, Caveman is more challenging version of Barbarian. They are
both
strong from beginning and very poor spellcasters. But in addition,
Caveman
does start with neither poison resistance nor good weapon. I played
lawful which means you get rather easy Excalibur -- playing neutral
removes
this possibility. Unlike Barbarian, Caveman gets quite good artifact
with
very useful hungerless conflict-generating capability, especially
useful in
the endgame.

